<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Severia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seversk land, Siveria, Sivershchyna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Severia was a historical region in the basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a> and Seim rivers, now divided between north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, named after the East Slavic Severians. Its towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a>, Starodub, Putivl and Rylsk. Held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> from the fourteenth century, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in the war of 1500–1503, was ceded to Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1618 and returned to Russia in the mid-seventeenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/severia.jpg" alt="Severia" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Maria Kuczyński · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziemie_Czernihowsko-Siewierskie_w_XV_wieku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Severia is a historical region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, its largest part lying in modern Russia and its central part around the city of Chernihiv in Ukraine. It took its name from the Severians, an East Slavic tribe of the late first millennium whose name means Northerners. Their main settlements included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a>, Rylsk, Starodub, Trubchevsk, Sevsk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bryansk">Bryansk</a> and Belgorod in modern Russia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Liubech</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novhorod-Siverskyi</a>, Chernihiv, Putyvl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hlukhiv">Hlukhiv</a> in modern Ukraine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> the Severians paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oleg-of-novgorod">Oleg of Novgorod</a> (reigned 879–912) conquered them and added their lands to the new principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> (1019–1054) they had lost most of their distinctness, and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a> came under Chernihiv. In 1096 Oleg I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> created a large Severian Principality, reaching the upper Oka, which served until the end of the century as a buffer against Cuman attacks. Its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a> is the subject of in the 12th-century epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-igors-campaign">The Tale of Igor's Campaign</a>.</p><p>After the Mongol invasion the principality was largely ruined, though it survived repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> invasions, and little is recorded of it in the 13th century. By the mid-14th century it was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, whose Gediminid princes, Ruthenian-speaking and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, made their capitals at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novhorod-Siverskyi</a>, Starodub and Trubchevsk. After the Lithuanian defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vedrosha">Battle of Vedrosha</a> the principality passed to Muscovy. Prince Vasiliy Shemiachich held it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> until he was imprisoned there in 1523, after which Muscovite voivodes governed it. In the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">Hetmans</a> of the Ukrainian Cossacks resided at Baturyn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hlukhiv">Hlukhiv</a> and Pochep, and Hlukhiv grew into a capital of 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the Severian lands, populated by a mixture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and Russians, were divided between the Ukrainian and Russian Soviet republics. A distinctive Severian style of icon painting formed from the 16th and 17th centuries, marked by restraint and asceticism under conservative Byzantine influence, and a collection of such icons is kept in the Museum of Ukrainian home icons in Radomysl Castle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Severia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wars with the Grand Duchy of Lithuania in 1487–1494 and 1500–1503, including the victory at the Battle of Vedrosha in 1500, won the Chernigov and <b>Seversk lands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The name <b>Siveria</b> survived for the region, and the Severians are last mentioned as a people in the 11th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/severians">Severians</a></li></ul></section>
